**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 20 in full with exercises; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005](../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG), the organic/bonding applications in the advanced sequence; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), the relevant carbon/bonding examples.

Organic chemistry uses the same atoms, bonding, geometry, energy, and reaction principles with a rich set of carbon-based structures. Learn structural notation, functional groups, and naming through identifiable molecular examples. A molecular formula may leave connectivity, stereochemistry, conformation, and speciation undetermined.

Connect a functional group with plausible interactions, acid/base behavior, reaction possibilities, and evidence needed to identify it. A label is a useful organizing cue, not a complete prediction of every molecule's reactivity, toxicity, or material behavior. Solvent, neighboring structure, catalysts, temperature, and competing pathways can matter.

Distinguish constitutional and stereochemical differences, and relate geometry to properties or recognition where supported. Use the earlier orbital/resonance models to explain a small set of examples rather than memorizing a large disconnected reaction list. Spectroscopic and other analytical evidence will later test proposed structures.

**Work:** translate between formula and structural representations for selected textbook species, identify functional groups, and explain what remains ambiguous. Compare two isomers using a defined property/evidence question. Write an atom/charge-balanced reaction account and distinguish it from an asserted mechanism.

Read the entire organic-introduction chapter and its exercises. The later full organic-chemistry trail will develop mechanism, synthesis, spectroscopy, stereochemistry, and research in depth; this Record provides a complete general-chemistry entrance and connections to solvents, polymers, fuels, biology, and industrial feedstocks.

